{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "ויקבר אותו בגי", :translit "vayikbor oto ba'gay", :gloss "And He buried him in the valley"} {:he "חסר אל\"ף", :translit "chaser alef", :gloss "defective [of the letter] alef"} {:he "שלא ידע איש את קבורתו", :translit "she'lo yada ish et kevurato", :gloss "that no man knew his burial"} {:he "מרע\"ה", :translit "mora'eh", :gloss "his teacher"} {:he "איש", :translit "ish", :gloss "man"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Spelling observation: בגי written without alef, linking the valley word to the phrase 'no man (ish) knew'"}], :frame {:text "The verse states that no man (איש) knew Moses' burial place (Deuteronomy 34:6). Moses himself is called איש at Numbers 12:3 ('Now the man Moses was very meek'), making the term broad enough to include him. The phrase יחידו של עולם, 'the Unique One of the world,' is a standard rabbinic designation for God found in classical sources such as Berakhot 7b and Ta'anit 3:8.", :sources ["Deuteronomy 34:6" "Numbers 12:3" "Berakhot 7b" "Ta'anit 3:8"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :other, :note "Spelling observation: בגי written without alef, linking the valley word to the phrase 'no man (ish) knew'", :by :selah, :ran? false}], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 34, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:41:28.838733235Z", :model "glm-5.2"}